     <item row="4" column="1">
      <widget class="DKFormPicker" name="color"/>
     </item>
     <item row="5" column="1">
      <widget class="DKFormPicker" name="textureSet">
       <property name="whatsThis">
        <string>An optional TextureSet that can be used to override the model's default textures.

In the vanilla game, there exists a separate HeadPart for every eye color. They all use the same few NIFs, relying on this TextureSet to override the texture and change the eye color.</string>
       </property>
      </widget>
     </item>
    </layout>
   </item>
   <item>
    <widget class="QGroupBox" name="groupBox">
     <property name="whatsThis">
      <string>&lt;p&gt;Ordinarily, a HeadPart can only specify a single model (and morphs for it). If you need to use multiple NIFs, the solution is to create one HeadPart for each NIF: have the main HeadPart list the others as &quot;extra parts,&quot; and have those others be flagged as &quot;extra parts.&quot;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;As an example: several hairstyles in the vanilla game have two HeadParts: a &quot;hair&quot; model, and a &quot;hairline&quot; model. The hairline model is molded directly to the character's head and uses alpha blending. The hairline is flagged as an extra part, and is listed in the hair part's &quot;extra parts&quot; list.&lt;/p&gt;</string>
     </property>
     <property name="title">
      <string>Extra Parts</string>
     </property>
     <layout class="QVBoxLayout" name="verticalLayout">
      <item>
       <widget class="QCheckBox" name="flagIsExtraPart">
        <property name="text">
         <string>Is Extra Part</string>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
       <widget class="DKFormListPane" name="extraParts">
        <property name="showFormTypes">
         <bool>false</bool>
        </property>
        <property name="showMoveButtons">
         <bool>true</bool>
        </property>
       </widget>
      </item>
     </layout>
    </widget>
   </item>
